{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-341825" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the instantaneous rate of change of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-{x}^{2}$ when $x=0.$\n\n${f}^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-341825" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The instantaneous rate of change when $x=0$ is given by the value of the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ at $x=0.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the derivative, using the fact that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})=2x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡x-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & ={sec}^{2}⁡(0)-2\\cdot 0 \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)})}^{2}-0 \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{1})}^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change is $1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-341825" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1a6558b688184560a1bde6dc4c7f0a757e0d73b8067a1f22bae8ed220db4e48a"} {"question" "q-341825" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-341825" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-1801" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate $(\\frac{10}{6}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-1801" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, note that $(\\frac{10}{6})$ means the same thing as ${}_{10}{C}_{6}.$\n\n In general, the number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=10$ and $r=6,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{10}{C}_{6} & =\\frac{10!}{(10-6)!6!} \\\\  & =\\frac{10!}{4!6!} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\cdot 9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7\\cdot \\cancel{6!}}{4!\\cancel{6!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\cdot 9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7}{4\\cdot 3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\,040}{24} \\\\  & =210.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-1801" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1a6558b688184560a1bde6dc4c7f0a757e0d73b8067a1f22bae8ed220db4e48a"} {"question" "q-1801" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-1801" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "180"] ["math" "196"] ["math" "1\\,080"] ["math" "210"] ["math" "5\\,040"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-1801" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "210"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-27448" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/assets/f5b33723509f58055f0ded0c575ea097cc90ff05fcfc72fc3270553248de0f2b.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y={(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}+c.$ Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-27448" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The horizontal asymptote of $y={(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}$ is $y=0.$ \n\nThe vertical translation $y={(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-3.$ Therefore, $c=-3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-27448" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1a6558b688184560a1bde6dc4c7f0a757e0d73b8067a1f22bae8ed220db4e48a"} {"question" "q-27448" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-27448" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y={(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}"] ["math" "y={(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}+2"] ["math" "y={(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}+3"] ["math" "y={(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}-2"] ["math" "y={(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}-3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-27448" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y={(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}-3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-6912" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2x}{3{x}^{2}+1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-6912" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}+1=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions. Therefore, $f(x)$ does not have any vertical asymptotes." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-6912" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1a6558b688184560a1bde6dc4c7f0a757e0d73b8067a1f22bae8ed220db4e48a"} {"question" "q-6912" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-6912" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=-3,\\,x=3"] ["math" "x=-3,\\,x=\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "x=-\\frac{1}{3},\\,x=3"] ["math" "x=-\\frac{1}{3},\\,x=\\frac{1}{3}"] ["text" "The function has no vertical asymptotes"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-6912" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The function has no vertical asymptotes"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-64730" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/assets/2f01b946a5c7c0f93c2171379603c4426913af5a15d0bc03434785144cfe4102.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},$ what is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-64730" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{2}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. Therefore $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nUsing the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we finally get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}=1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-64730" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1a6558b688184560a1bde6dc4c7f0a757e0d73b8067a1f22bae8ed220db4e48a"} {"question" "q-64730" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-64730" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-64730" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-340690" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ when $f(x)=(1-7x)^{4}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-340690" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf={g}^{4},\\,g=1-7x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=4{g}^{3},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=-7.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =4{g}^{3}\\cdot (-7) \\\\  & =-28(1-7x)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-340690" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1a6558b688184560a1bde6dc4c7f0a757e0d73b8067a1f22bae8ed220db4e48a"} {"question" "q-340690" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-340690" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-28(1-7x)^3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-108605" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/assets/a6e311063c3222d8be1b5fe37379d21c4dbb3198afd7c338a273bb1cf366fc1e.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-108605" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.\n\nFrom the above, we see that:\n\n- For graph II, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, graph II shows a one-to-one function. As a result, this function is invertible.\n\n- For graphs I, III, and IV, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. These are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-108605" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1a6558b688184560a1bde6dc4c7f0a757e0d73b8067a1f22bae8ed220db4e48a"} {"question" "q-108605" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-108605" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and IV only"] ["text" "I, III, and IV only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-108605" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-3304" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${8}^{x}\\cdot {4}^{2x-1}=32.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-3304" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base. To do this, we rewrite the equation using $8={2}^{3}$, $4={2}^{2}$ and $32={2}^{5},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{({2}^{3})}^{x}\\cdot {({2}^{2})}^{2x-1}={2}^{5}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule and the product rule of exponents. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{3x}\\cdot {2}^{2(2x-1)} & ={2}^{5} \\\\ {2}^{3x+2(2x-1)} & ={2}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(2)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2(2x-1) & =5 \\\\ 3x+4x-2 & =5 \\\\ 7x & =7 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-3304" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1a6558b688184560a1bde6dc4c7f0a757e0d73b8067a1f22bae8ed220db4e48a"} {"question" "q-3304" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-3304" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-3304" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-49235" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that ${\\int }_{e}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+\\frac{ie}{n})\\cdot \\frac{e}{n}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-49235" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=e.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{e}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{e}{n}=\\frac{b-e}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=2e.\n$$\n\nConsequently,  ${x}_{i}=e+\\frac{ie}{n}$ and $f({x}_{i})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{e}^{2e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+\\frac{ie}{n})\\cdot \\frac{e}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and $b=2e.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-49235" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1a6558b688184560a1bde6dc4c7f0a757e0d73b8067a1f22bae8ed220db4e48a"} {"question" "q-49235" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-49235" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+e)$ and $b=2e$"] ["text" "$f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+e)$ and $b=e+1$"] ["text" "$f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+e)$ and $b={e}^{2}$"] ["text" "$f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and $b=2e$"] ["text" "$f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and $b={e}^{2}$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-49235" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and $b=2e$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-78131" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/assets/3c0dc4b8f7f45a5bc1177778431eccf9bcaac1bf6d520d605f9e6bc08d5b1d91.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\n- $x=-2$\n- $x=0$\n- $x=2$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-78131" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-2.$ Since there is an asymptote at $x=-2,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=-2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=0$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-78131" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1a6558b688184560a1bde6dc4c7f0a757e0d73b8067a1f22bae8ed220db4e48a"} {"question" "q-78131" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-78131" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"} {"question" "q-78131" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a2055a5ee9fe4928eafced4a47aa7d7bbfdb201cfa67cec2c796f0b9657f39b"}] "usage" {"q-1801" []} "basis" 876 "retractions" [[17592186083493 :question/problem "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nEvaluate $(\\frac{10}{6})$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186080289 17592186080290 17592186080291 17592186080292 17592186080293] "immutable_fields" [[17592186080288 :answer-field/correct] [17592186080288 :answer-field/id] [17592186080288 :answer-field/key] [17592186080288 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
